A["ece641/final/em/2"] = R`<p><b>Tutor: what this problem is really about.</b> Running the EM template on an observation model you have not been drilled on. The lesson is that the template does not care which exponential family the observations come from: find the natural sufficient statistics, and everything else is mechanical. Bernoulli vectors also teach a practical point: products of many probabilities underflow, so posteriors are computed in the log domain.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: how I would walk you through it.</b></p><ol><li>Write $p(y_n|x_n=m)$ as a product over the $D$ coordinates; independence given the class is what makes it factor.</li><li>Take the log and collect terms: you will see $\sum_ny_{n,d}\delta(x_n=m)$ and $\sum_n\delta(x_n=m)$ appear. Those are your sufficient statistics $b_{m,d}$ and $N_m$.</li><li>Complete-data ML: maximize with respect to $q_{m,d}$ for fixed class counts; each is a fraction of ones. Class priors come from a Lagrange multiplier as usual.</li><li>E-step: Bayes' rule with the current parameters; compute log-likelihoods, subtract the maximum before exponentiating.</li><li>M-step: the same formulas with expected counts. Iterate until the parameters stop changing.</li><li>Ask what could go wrong: a $q_{m,d}$ of exactly 0 or 1 makes a log infinite; a small floor or a Beta prior fixes it.</li></ol>
<p><b>Tutor: how to check your own work before looking at the solution.</b> Every $\hat q_{m,d}$ must lie in $[0,1]$ and every $\hat\pi_m$ must sum to one. Try $D=1$ and $M=2$: you should recover EM for a mixture of two coins, which you can reason about by hand.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: where else this way of thinking applies.</b> Binary image segmentation, clustering documents by word presence, and the M-step of any model whose likelihood factors over coordinates. The same reasoning with a Gaussian in each coordinate gives the diagonal-covariance Gaussian mixture.</p>`;
A["ece641/final/mc/2"] = R`<p><b>Tutor: what this problem is really about.</b> The logical structure of stationarity and reversibility. Students often treat detailed balance, full balance and reversibility as three separate facts; they are one idea seen from three sides. Detailed balance is a statement about pairs of states, full balance about single states, and reversibility about the joint law of two consecutive states.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: how I would walk you through it.</b></p><ol><li>Write the detailed balance equation and sum both sides over $i$. Use $\sum_iP_{j,i}=1$ to see the full balance equation appear. This proves DBE implies FBE.</li><li>To see the converse fails, keep the biased ring in mind: uniform $\pi$ satisfies FBE, but $\pi_ip\ne\pi_jq$.</li><li>Reversibility: write $P\{X_{n-1}=i,X_n=j\}=\pi_iP_{i,j}$ by the chain rule; swapping $i$ and $j$ gives the other joint probability. Their equality is exactly DBE.</li><li>For $\lim P^n$, interpret $[P^n]_{i,j}$ as the probability of being in $j$ after $n$ steps from $i$; ergodicity says this forgets $i$.</li><li>Symmetric $P$: columns sum to one, so uniform $\pi$ works, and DBE holds trivially because $P_{i,j}=P_{j,i}$.</li></ol>
<p><b>Tutor: how to check your own work before looking at the solution.</b> Every proof here is two or three lines; if yours is longer you are probably re-deriving something the definitions already give you. Also confirm that each equation you write has the same free indices on both sides.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: where else this way of thinking applies.</b> Every MCMC correctness argument is "show DBE with the target, then conclude stationarity." The reversed chain $Q$ appears in importance sampling and in the analysis of Hastings–Metropolis with asymmetric proposals.</p>`;
A["ece641/final/mcmc/1"] = R`<p><b>Tutor: what this problem is really about.</b> Seeing Metropolis, Hastings–Metropolis and Gibbs sampling as one construction: propose from any $q$, then accept with a ratio chosen so that detailed balance holds with the target. Once you see this, the Gibbs sampler is no longer a separate algorithm; it is the special case whose proposal is already correct and needs no rejection.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: how I would walk you through it.</b></p><ol><li>Write the Hastings ratio and note that the target appears only as a ratio, so the normalizing constant cancels; this is the whole reason MCMC exists.</li><li>Prove DBE by cases on which side of the ratio is larger; in each case one acceptance probability is 1 and the other is the ratio.</li><li>Plug in $q(w|x)=p(w)$: the ratio is identically 1, the chain is exact after one step. Ask why this is useless in practice (if you could sample $p$ directly you would not need MCMC).</li><li>For the Gibbs sampler, write the proposal as "pick coordinate $J$, draw from the conditional." Show the ratio is 1 using $p(w)=p_J(w_J|x_{\setminus J})p(x_{\setminus J})$.</li><li>Prove stationarity of Gibbs directly through the FBE to see the same fact from the other side.</li></ol>
<p><b>Tutor: how to check your own work before looking at the solution.</b> The acceptance probability must be in $[0,1]$ and must equal 1 whenever the proposal moves toward higher target probability with a symmetric $q$. If your Gibbs ratio is not exactly 1, you have mixed up $x_J$ and $w_J$ in the conditional.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: where else this way of thinking applies.</b> Designing samplers for MRF priors, simulated annealing, Bayesian uncertainty quantification in imaging, and diffusion-model samplers, which are Langevin chains corrected by the same detailed-balance idea.</p>`;
A["ece641/final/mlfinal/0"] = R`<p><b>Tutor: what this problem is really about.</b> The maximum-likelihood routine for the two one-parameter distributions the final uses most. The deeper point: both are exponential families whose natural sufficient statistic is the sample sum, so the ML estimate is a sample mean and its variance shrinks like $1/N$. If you can do this in three minutes you have time for the hard problems.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: how I would walk you through it.</b></p><ol><li>Write the density of one sample, take the negative log, and sum over samples. Circle the only data-dependent quantity: $S=\sum y_n$.</li><li>Rewrite the density as $\exp\{\eta T+d(\theta)+s(y)\}$ to name $\eta$ and $T$; this is one line and worth points.</li><li>Differentiate the NLL with respect to the parameter, set to zero, solve.</li><li>Confirm a minimum: second derivative positive at the solution, or the NLL tends to infinity at both ends of the parameter range.</li><li>Bias: take the expectation of your estimator using $E[Y]$. Variance: use independence to divide the single-sample variance by $N$.</li><li>Repeat for the Poisson case; notice the structure is identical.</li></ol>
<p><b>Tutor: how to check your own work before looking at the solution.</b> Units: the exponential mean estimate has the units of $y$; the Poisson rate estimate is dimensionless per interval. Both estimates must be sample means; if you get anything else, a log was dropped.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: where else this way of thinking applies.</b> Photon-count models in CT (Poisson), dwell-time and decay models (exponential), and the complete-data M-step of EM, which reuses these exact formulas with expected statistics.</p>`;
A["ece641/final/mmfinal/0"] = R`<p><b>Tutor: what this problem is really about.</b> When the symmetric-bound surrogate is legitimate, and what breaks at the origin for potentials with infinite curvature. This is the mathematics behind iteratively reweighted least squares for sparsity-promoting priors.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: how I would walk you through it.</b></p><ol><li>Compute $\rho'(\Delta)$, then the ratio $\rho'(\Delta)/\Delta$. The surrogate exists wherever this ratio is even and non-increasing in $|\Delta|$.</li><li>Write the parabola $\tfrac{\rho'(\Delta')}{2\Delta'}\Delta^2+c$ and choose $c$ so it touches $\rho$ at $\Delta'$.</li><li>Examine $\Delta'\to0$: the curvature $|\Delta'|^{p-2}$ blows up for $p&lt;2$, so no finite parabola bounds $\rho$ near the origin; state the standard fixes (clamp, or smooth the potential).</li><li>Assemble the surrogate for the full cost; it is a weighted least-squares problem whose minimizer is a weighted average.</li><li>Interpret the weights: small differences get large weight (strong smoothing), large differences get small weight (edges preserved).</li></ol>
<p><b>Tutor: how to check your own work before looking at the solution.</b> Set $p=2$: the weights must become constant and the update must be ordinary least squares. Let $p\to1$: weights approach $1/|\Delta'|$, the IRLS weights for the $\ell_1$ norm.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: where else this way of thinking applies.</b> Total-variation minimization by IRLS, robust regression with Huber or Cauchy losses, and any MM algorithm for a sum of concave-in-square potentials.</p>`;
A["ece641/final/icdl1/0"] = R`<p><b>Tutor: what this problem is really about.</b> Coordinate descent when the objective has a non-smooth but separable part. Two facts carry the problem: the one-coordinate subproblem is a scalar soft-threshold, and separability of the $\ell_1$ term makes coordinate-wise optimality sufficient for global optimality, which is false for general convex non-smooth functions.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: how I would walk you through it.</b></p><ol><li>Strict convexity: the quadratic term has Hessian $A^tA\succ0$ under full column rank; adding a convex term keeps strict convexity. Existence follows from coercivity.</li><li>Fix all coordinates but $x_i$, write the cost as a function of the new value $t$, complete the square using the residual $e$ and the column norm.</li><li>Recognize $\tfrac{c}{2}(t-\tilde t)^2+\lambda|t|$ and apply the soft threshold with level $\lambda/c$.</li><li>Update the residual incrementally so that each coordinate costs one column's worth of work.</li><li>Fixed point argument: coordinate-wise optimality means $0$ is in each partial subdifferential; because the non-smooth part is a sum over coordinates, the full subdifferential is the product of the partials, so $0\in\partial f$.</li></ol>
<p><b>Tutor: how to check your own work before looking at the solution.</b> With $\lambda=0$ your update must reduce to the ordinary least-squares ICD step. The threshold level must scale inversely with the column energy: columns with large norm are thresholded less.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: where else this way of thinking applies.</b> Coordinate-descent solvers for LASSO (glmnet), sparse-view CT with $\ell_1$ priors, and understanding why coordinate descent fails for non-separable non-smooth terms such as total variation.</p>`;
A["ece641/final/genrv/0"] = R`<p><b>Tutor: what this problem is really about.</b> A four-line proof that every simulation textbook starts with, and the reason it matters: any distribution with a computable inverse CDF can be sampled from uniform random numbers. The step students miss is why you may apply $F$ to both sides of an inequality: monotonicity.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: how I would walk you through it.</b></p><ol><li>Write the event $\{F^{-1}(U)\le\lambda\}$; because $F$ is increasing, it equals $\{U\le F(\lambda)\}$.</li><li>Use the uniform CDF: $P\{U\le t\}=t$ for $t\in[0,1]$. Done.</li><li>For concrete distributions, solve $F(x)=u$ for $x$; note that $1-U$ is also uniform, which simplifies signs.</li><li>Discrete distributions: the CDF is a staircase; the inverse is "first index whose cumulative sum exceeds $U$."</li><li>Gaussian vectors: whiten in reverse, $X=E\Lambda^{1/2}W$; verify the covariance.</li></ol>
<p><b>Tutor: how to check your own work before looking at the solution.</b> Your samples must land in the support (non-negative for exponential and Rayleigh) and their mean must match the distribution's mean when you plug in $E[-\ln U]=1$.</p>
<p><b>Tutor: where else this way of thinking applies.</b> Generating proposals for Metropolis, drawing class labels in Gibbs sampling, and producing latent vectors for generative networks.</p>`;
